90 The foundation of any technological innovation lies in its architecture, and the 91 Smart Farming System under consideration is no exception. This section meticulously 92 dissects the interconnected modules that form the backbone of the proposed system, 93 orchestrating a symphony of data collection, processing, analysis, and actionable 94 insights. 95 96 97 98 99 100 101 102 103 104 105 106
107 Fig. 1. Proposed System Architecture 108
109 A. Wireless Sensor Network (WSN) Module
110 At the core of the Smart Farming System is the Wireless Sensor Network 111 (WSN) module. Deployed strategically across farmlands, an array of sensors 112 serves as the frontline data gatherers. These sensors, ranging from soil moisture 113 detectors to temperature and humidity sensors, create a real-time data stream that 114 captures the nuances of the agricultural environment. This module facilitates 115 continuous monitoring, offering a dynamic understanding of soil conditions and 116 crop health. 117
118 B. Data Processing and Edge Computing Module
119 The influx of data from the WSN module is then channeled to the Data 120 Processing and Edge Computing Module. Here, the power of edge computing is 121 harnessed to perform initial data processing closer to the data source. This 122 strategic approach minimizes latency, conserves bandwidth, and enables swift 123 decision-making. Within this module, raw sensor data undergoes preprocessing, 124 noise filtration, and basic analytics. The output is a refined dataset ready for the 125 next stages of analysis. 126
127 C. Cloud Computing Module
128 Following the initial processing, the pre-processed data is seamlessly 129 transmitted to the Cloud Computing Module, constituting the backbone of the 130 Smart Farming System. Leveraging cloud platforms such as Amazon Web 131 Services (AWS) or Microsoft Azure, this module stores, manages, and processes 132 vast amounts of agricultural data. The cloud infrastructure provides scalable
